    "body": "### Related\r\n* Follows https://github.com/rerun-io/rerun/pull/9846\r\n* Closes https://github.com/rerun-io/rerun/issues/9845\r\n* https://github.com/rerun-io/rerun/issues/9818\r\n\r\n### What\r\nThis PR rewrites our flushing logic to be more robust and configurable.\r\n\r\nWhen flushing a recording stream or a sink, you can now configure the timeout. The default timeout is \u221e, meaning the call will block until it either succeeds or fails.\r\n\r\nThis should solve some issues we've seen with data getting dropped because the flush was too impatient (only waited 2-3 seconds).\r\n\r\nThe flushing will also now break early if the gRPC connection is not connected, regardless of timeout.\r\nThis means the flushing will only block IF there is some hope of progress (the connection is open).\r\n\r\n### Previous behavior\r\nPreviously we would drop data if flushing a sink took more than 2-3 seconds (depending on the code path).\r\nWhen logging a lot of data in a short amount of time (e.g. logging a lot of images), we can easily hit this timeout.\r\n\r\nThe timeout time could be configured, but only for the gRPC sink, and was awkwardly done at initialization time (instead of when calling `flush`).\r\n\r\n### Example output\r\n```\r\n[2025-08-26T16:15:12Z WARN  re_grpc_client::message_proxy::write] Flushing the gRPC stream has taken over 20s seconds; will keep waiting\u2026\r\n[2025-08-26T16:15:28Z INFO  re_grpc_client::message_proxy::write] Flush completed in 35.9 seconds\r\n```\r\n\r\n### TODO\r\n* [x] Add notice in migration guide\r\n* [x] \u221e is not great during shutdown, as an invalid gRPC URL keeps `RecordingStream::drop` running forever\r\n* [x] If a `flush_blocking` method has a timeout argument, call it `flush` instead ?\r\n* [x] Return different error codes in C++ and Python for flush timeout and for flush error - allows backpressure\r\n* [x] `@rerun-bot full-check`\r\n* [x] The Python `flush` function will now raise an exception if it fails. DO WE WANT THIS?\r\n\r\n### Future work\r\n* Add an API for discarding data? Maybe `flush(timeout=10.0, discard_on_timeout=True)` ?",
